{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":1,"query":"name=Zakaria&limit=10&title=From Wealth to Power","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":{"titleInfo":{"title":"From Wealth to Power","subTitle":"The Unusual Origins of America's World Role"},"name":{"@nameTitleGroup":"1","@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Zakaria, Fareed.","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":"text","originInfo":[{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nju"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New Jersey"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso3166","#text":"US-NJ"}}],"dateIssued":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"1999"},"edition":"1st ed.","issuance":"monographic"},{"@eventType":"publication","place":{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Princeton, N.J. :"}},"publisher":"Princeton University Press,","dateIssued":[1999]},{"@eventType":"manufacture","place":{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Baltimore, Md. :"}},"publisher":"Project MUSE,","dateOther":{"@type":"manufacture","#text":"2021"}}],"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":[{"@authority":"marccategory","#text":"electronic resource"},{"@authority":"marcsmd","#text":"remote"},{"@type":"media","@authority":"","#text":"computer"},{"@type":"carrier","@authority":"","#text":"online resource"}],"extent":"1 online resource (346 p.)"},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"What turns rich nations into great powers? How do wealthy countries begin extending their influence abroad? These questions are vital to understanding one of the most important sources of instability in international politics: the emergence of a new power. In From Wealth to Power, Fareed Zakaria seeks to answer these questions by examining the most puzzling case of a rising power in modern history--that of the United States. If rich nations routinely become great powers, Zakaria asks, then how do we explain the strange inactivity of the United States in the late nineteenth century? B."},"tableOfContents":"Introduction : what makes a great power? -- A theory of foreign policy : why do states expand? -- Imperial understretch : power and nonexpansion, 1865-1889 -- The rise of the American state, 1877-1896 : the foundation for a new foreign policy -- The new diplomacy, 1889-1908 : the emergence of a great power -- Conclusion : strong nation, weak state.","note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Fareed Zakaria."},"Originally published: 1998.","\"First paperback printing, 1999\"--Title page verso.",{"@type":"additional physical form","#text":"Issued also in print."},{"@type":"language","#text":"English"}],"subject":[{"@authority":"bisacsh","topic":"HISTORY / United States / 19th Century"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"International relations"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Diplomatic relations"},{"@authority":"bisacsh","topic":["POLITICAL SCIENCE","International Relations","General"]},{"@authority":"bisacsh","topic":["POLITICAL SCIENCE","Government","International"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"International relations"},{"@authority":"fast","geographic":"United States"},{"@authority":"lcsh","geographic":"United States","topic":"Foreign relations","temporal":"1865-1921"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"E661.7 .Z35 2001"},{"@authority":"ddc","#text":"327.73"}],"relatedItem":[{"titleInfo":{"title":"Princeton paperbacks"}},{"titleInfo":{"title":"Princeton studies in international history and politics"}},null,null,null,null,{"@type":"series","titleInfo":{"title":"Princeton Studies in International History and Politics"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/99155654326903941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9781400829187"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"1400829186"},{"@type":"doi","#text":"10.1515/97